the government's insisting that it is  acting within international law after  publishing legislation to override parts  of the brexit deal the foreign secretary  says the uk is still open to  negotiations with the european union  despite making moves to ignore the  northern ireland protocol  we're very clear that we're acting in  line with the law we'll be publishing  our legal statement later today what is  vitally important  is that we do resolve this situation in  northern ireland that is causing real  problems we haven't seen the executive  operating since february we need to get  power sharing re-established we know how  hard one the belfast good friday  agreement was and that is why the  government has to act  that is why we are introducing this  legislation now of course we remain open  to negotiations with the eu and i would  rather achieve this through a negotiated  settlement the fact is the eu  the eu have refused to change the  protocol which is causing these problems  on trade on tax  and more broadly in northern ireland  well the move has been welcomed by the  democratic unionists but shin fein says  it's a clear violation of the law  i believe that uh finally we are now  seeing the kind of  action that is required  uh to begin the process of removing the  barriers for trade within the united  kingdom  to restoring northern ireland's place  within the uk internal market as the  bill progresses we will consider what  steps we can take along with others to  build  progress to make progress in northern  ireland i want these institutions up and  running but i'm also clear  we need to be sure that this legislation  is going forward  today's action by boris johnson in  westminster is absolutely reckless it's  disgraceful  it does nothing to serve the interests  of the people here it flies in the face  of an international agreement which he  himself negotiated it is unclear breach  of international law  and it is you know the reality is here  that the protocol is working the  majority of businesses have been out and  be very vocal on that 80 percent of  businesses would say the protocol is  working it's actually affording them  great opportunities it's creating jobs  and yet and all we have boris johnson  again delivering on  the commitment that he made to breach  international law i don't think that's a  good look on anybody  michelle o'neill there we've got  correspondence in westminster belfast  and brussels let's uh go first to  westminster and rob powell so  clearly rob a controversial  move by the government just remind us  why they claim this is necessary  well they say it's necessary because  they think there are problems that are  being caused in northern ireland by the  northern ireland protocol that means  that the uk needs to take action on its  own to amend the protocol and they're  going to do that through a bill that's  just been published this is it the  northern ireland protocol bill  and it is 20 pages long and if you just  read basically the first main chunk of  it that has the overview of the main  provisions you get a sense of what it's  designed to do it said this act provides  that certain specified provisions of the  northern ireland protocol does not have  effect in the united kingdom and it  gives ministers of the crown powers to  make new law in connection with the  northern ireland protocol so really as  suspected for a long time a bill that  will be probably anyway passed through  the commons and the lords that will give  ministers here in westminster power to  uh rewrite to make new law in regards to  the northern ireland protocol to  disapply parts of the northern ireland  protocol  as well  in terms of specifically what that will  be well from the briefings we've been  having with the government today they  say that it's essentially focused on  four main parts the first is creating  this green and red channel system for  products coming into northern ireland so  that  goods that come through the green  channel which are not at risk not likely  to be moved on to the eu don't have to  have so much paperwork and so many  checks whilst the red goods still would  have full checks the second is about  regulation about standards and about  allowing goods into northern ireland  making them saying that they can either  abide by the eu regime or the uk regime  so a dual regime essentially um in in  northern ireland the third is about the  application of vat and state aid rules  and the fourth is about governance  essentially about what role european  courts have in  arbitrating disputes in northern ireland  the uk wants there to be an independent  arbitration process which can just have  reference to european court so european  courts wouldn't get the final say on  disputes um in northern ireland  in terms of the legal position in all of  this um the government has also  published online a statement of what  they say is their legal underpinning for  their um justification that this does  not in their view breach international  law and they are relying on something  called the doctrine of necessity where  essentially they say  it provides a clear basis and i'm  reading to you from their justification  here on online a clear basis in  international law to justify the  non-performance of international  obligations under certain exceptional  and limited circumstances now the  exceptional limited circumstances that  they are relying on that they are  talking about is  what they see as the political the  social impact that the northern ireland  protocol is having on life in northern  ireland so essentially they believe the  situation being caused by the protocol  in northern ireland is so severe that it  crosses the threshold and gives them  grounds to disapply parts of the brexit  deal parts of the northern ireland  protocol that boris johnson signed some  two and a half years ago now i think  what we will see over the coming hours  days and probably weeks are  international law experts that have a  much much much more um uh well-versed in  this than i could ever be uh unpick this  bill unpick the legalese in this bill um  and come to their own decision about  whether um they think this does indeed  breach international law the  government's position at the moment  though is that they don't believe it  does but of course there have been  disputes over which top government  lawyers have had their say over this and  accusations essentially of the  government shopping around for legal  advice that fits their political aim so  i think we will see that row over the  legal grounding for this and play out in  the coming days but the end result of  what's happened in the last hour is that  this 20-page bill the northern ireland  protocol bill has been introduced into  the house of commons it's had its first  reading a relatively quick procedural uh  process that goes on in the commons it  will be back in a couple of weeks time  probably for its second reading it's at  that point we will get a lot more anger  from the green benches and potentially a  rebellion from conservative mps as well  okay rob thanks very much let's go to uh  belfast and uh stephen murphy and we  heard at the top of the programme uh  there stephen uh  welcomed by the unionists and condemned  by sinn fein  doctrine of necessity as rob uh was  explaining there it doesn't really cut  much mustard here with the nationalist  community the nationalist parties at  storm and certainly didn't cut any  mustard with michelle o'neill i directly  asked her is what boris johnson's doing  today  illegal and she said yes she said  definitively yes it breaches  international law breaches uh the brexit  withdrawal agreement and the protocol  obviously contained uh within uh she is  furious she called it reckless and she  called it a dangerous move that serves  nobody it doesn't serve the people here  in northern ireland she said and it  doesn't serve the businesses either what  